To calculate the boiling point, we use the Clausius-Clapeyron equation, which is $\frac{\Delta H_{\text {vaporization }}}{T_{b}^{2}}=\frac{R}{V} \ln \left(\frac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}\right)$. Rearranging this equation to solve for $T_{b}$, we get $T_{b}=\sqrt{\frac{\Delta H_{\text {vaporization }}}{R} \ln \left(\frac{P_{2}}{P_{1}}\right)}$. Plugging in the given values, we get $T_{b}=\sqrt{\frac{40.656 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}} \ln \left(\frac{253 \mathrm{~Torr}}{1 \mathrm{~atm}}\right)}$. Converting torr to atm, we get $T_{b}=\sqrt{\frac{40.656 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}} \ln \left(\frac{253}{760 \mathrm{~atm}}\right)}$. Now, we can plug in the values and calculate: $T_{b}=\sqrt{\frac{40.656 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}} \ln \left(\frac{253}{760}\right)} \approx \sqrt{\frac{40656 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}} \times 2.84} \approx \sqrt{4893.8 \mathrm{~K}} \approx 70.7 \mathrm{~K}$. Since we know that the normal boiling point of water is $373.12 \mathrm{~K}$, we can find the boiling point of water at the top of Mt. Everest by adding $70.7 \mathrm{~K}$ to this value. So, the boiling point is $373.12 \mathrm{~K}+70.7 \mathrm{~K}=443.82 \mathrm{~K}$. Rounding to the nearest whole number, the boiling point of water on the top of Mt. Everest is $444 \mathrm{~K}$. The closest answer choice is 443 K
